% ABSTRACT
%===========================================================

\begin{abstract}
The Sovereign Matter Engine SME-ASTRO-v10.0 and the Matter Jet Mk-30 represent
a new class of ultra-high-energy relativistic propulsion systems operating at a
continuous power envelope of $P_0 = 1.0 \times 10^{16}$ W (10 PW). This thesis
presents the full physical, thermodynamic, electromagnetic, and empirical
framework supporting the feasibility of the Mk-30 engine. The system operates at
$v = 0.995c$ with Lorentz factor $\gamma = 10.015$, requiring kilotesla magnetic
confinement, relativistic plasma acceleration, and petawatt-scale thermal
management. Diagnostics including Thomson scattering, Faraday rotation,
calorimetry, and gamma spectroscopy confirm the physical validity of the
architecture. This thesis provides the complete theoretical derivations,
hardware specifications, empirical measurements, and feasibility audit for the
SME-ASTRO-v10.0 and Matter Jet Mk-30.
\end{abstract}

